U='https://dam.assets.ohio.gov/image/upload/epa.ohio.gov/Portals/35/pti/10wastewaterstandards-2014.pdf'
S='Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities (Ten States Standards), 2014 edition, Sections 33.1-33.8 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('std-10ss-slope-1','US','Ten States Standards minimum sewer slopes by pipe size (8 inch = 0.40 percent) and 2 feet per second',
"Ten States Standards (Recommended Standards for Wastewater Facilities, 2014 edition, Section 33). A public gravity sewer carrying raw wastewater is not less than 8 inches in diameter (33.1). Sewers are designed for a mean velocity of at least 2.0 feet per second flowing full (33.41). Recommended minimum slopes, in feet per 100 feet: 8 inch 0.40; 10 inch 0.28; 12 inch 0.22; 15 inch 0.15; 18 inch 0.12; 21 inch 0.10; 24 inch 0.08; 27 inch 0.067; 30 inch 0.058; 33 inch 0.052; 36 inch 0.046; 39 inch 0.041; 42 inch 0.037. Sewers 48 inches and larger are designed for at least 3.0 feet per second. Slightly flatter slopes can be allowed where the flow depth will be 0.3 of the diameter or more at design average flow (33.42). Installation (33.8): rigid pipe uses bedding Classes A, B, C or crushed stone as described in ASTM C12; flexible pipe uses embedment Classes I, II or III (ASTM D2321), carefully compacted; no debris, frozen material, large clods or stones, or organic matter in the final backfill within 2 feet of the top of the pipe (33.84). A crew does not pick the slope: lay to the plan grade, and flag it to the foreman or engineer if a plan grade is flatter than these.",S,U,'2014')

U='https://www.uni-bell.org/portals/0/resourcefile/installation-guide-for-solid-wall-pvc-sewer-pipe.pdf'
S='Uni-Bell PVC Pipe Association, Installation Guide for Solid-Wall PVC Sewer Pipe, PUB-06-25 (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('mfr-pvcpa-sewer-1','US','PVC sewer pipe (SDR 35, SDR 26, ASTM D3034): installation basics from the PVC Pipe Association guide',
"PVC gravity sewer pipe installation, summarized from the Uni-Bell PVC Pipe Association guide for solid-wall PVC sewer pipe (PUB-06-25). Product standard is ASTM D3034; the guide notes that since 2024 D3034 covers 3 through 60 inch, so old references to ASTM F679 for 18 inch and up are being replaced. Common stiffness classes: DR 35 and DR 26 in 4 to 15 inch; PS 46, PS 75 and PS 115 in 18 to 60 inch. Installation practice is ASTM D2321. Nothing larger than 1-1/2 inches should touch the pipe. Bedding is typically 4 to 6 inches; dig bell holes only as big as needed; bring an over-dug bottom back to grade for the full length, not just at the bells. Haunching: work material under the haunches and at least halfway up the pipe and compact it (shovel slicing works). Initial backfill: at least 6 inches over the pipe, rock free, before any machine compaction over the top. Flotation: keep the trench dry until there is about 2 pipe diameters of backfill over thin-wall pipe (DR 32.5 and higher) or 1-1/2 diameters over heavier pipe. Joints: lubricate per the maker, push until the insertion line is even with the bell lip, and do not over-insert; re-bevel and re-mark field cuts. Bending: rule of thumb radius is 200 times the pipe OD, and bending pipe over 12 inch is not recommended; do not bend and deflect the joint on the same length. Acceptance: clean, then CCTV; a go/no-go mandrel sized for 7-1/2 percent deflection per D3034 (many specs require 5 percent); low-pressure air per ASTM F1417.",S,U,'PUB-06-25','medium')

U='https://pe.mcwane.com/upl/downloads/dipra/dip-installation-guide-ab36416e.pdf'
S='Ductile Iron Pipe Research Association (DIPRA), Installation Guide for Ductile Iron Pipe, revised May 2015, hosted by McWane (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('mfr-dipra-joint-1','US','Ductile iron pipe joints: mechanical joint bolt torque and maximum joint deflection (DIPRA guide)',
"Ductile iron pipe joints, summarized from the DIPRA Installation Guide for Ductile Iron Pipe (revised May 2015). Mechanical joint bolt torque (Table 4): 5/8 inch bolts on 3 inch joints 45 to 60 ft-lb; 3/4 inch bolts on 4 to 24 inch 75 to 90 ft-lb; 1 inch bolts on 30 to 36 inch 100 to 120 ft-lb; 1-1/4 inch bolts on 42 to 48 inch 120 to 150 ft-lb. Tighten in a star pattern and go around again until every bolt is in range. Push-on joints: clean the bell groove and the spigot, seat the gasket, lubricate the gasket and the plain end (not the socket), bevel field cuts, push straight home and deflect only after the joint is made. Maximum push-on deflection on an 18-foot length (Table 5): 5 degrees, about 19 inches of offset, for 3 to 12 inch; 3 degrees, about 11 inches, for 14 to 42 inch. Mechanical joints (Table 6): 6 inch 7 degrees (27 inches); 8 to 12 inch 5 degrees (20 inches); 14 inch 3.5 degrees; 24 inch about 2 degrees. Cutting: abrasive saw (about a minute per inch of diameter), then grind and bevel the end. Check the pipe maker's own table for the exact joint: restrained joints allow less.",S,U,'2015')
add('mfr-dipra-install-1','US','Ductile iron pipe: laying conditions Types 1 to 5, cover, trench width, polywrap (DIPRA guide)',
"Ductile iron pipe laying, summarized from the DIPRA Installation Guide for Ductile Iron Pipe (revised May 2015). Laying conditions (the trench types used in AWWA C150 and C600): Type 1 flat-bottom trench with loose backfill (not for 14 inch and larger); Type 2 flat-bottom trench, backfill lightly consolidated to the pipe centerline; Type 3 pipe bedded in at least 4 inches of loose soil, backfill lightly consolidated to the top of the pipe; Type 4 bedding of sand, gravel or crushed stone to 1/8 of the pipe diameter (4 inch minimum), backfill compacted to about 80 percent Standard Proctor; Type 5 bedded to the centerline in compacted granular material (4 inch minimum under the pipe), backfill compacted to about 90 percent Standard Proctor. The plans say which type. Cover is generally 2.5 to 3 feet minimum, deeper in frost country. Keep rock at least 6 inches away from pipe 24 inch and smaller and 9 inches from 30 inch and larger. Polyethylene encasement (AWWA C105) in corrosive soil: one tube per pipe length, overlapped about 1 foot at joints, taped at the ends and about every 2 feet, with slack at the joints; repair tears with tape. Before tapping polywrapped pipe, wrap 2 to 3 layers of tape around the pipe at the tap. Direct taps: 3/4 inch on all sizes, 1 inch on 6 inch and larger; bigger services need a saddle or tapping sleeve per the guide's table.",S,U,'2015')
add('mfr-dipra-test-1','US','Water main pressure test, flushing and chlorination basics (DIPRA guide, AWWA C600 and C651)',
"Testing a new ductile iron water main, summarized from the DIPRA Installation Guide (revised May 2015), which follows AWWA C600 and C651. Pressure test: at least 1.5 times the working pressure at the lowest point of the section, held for at least 2 hours (150 psi working means a 225 psi test). Measure the make-up water needed to hold pressure; examples of the testing allowance at 150 psi are about 0.50 gallon per hour per 1,000 feet of 6 inch and 0.99 for 12 inch. Brace or restrain temporary plugs and caps. Flush at 3.0 feet per second: roughly 400 gpm for a 6 inch main and 1,060 gpm for a 12 inch. Chlorination (AWWA C651): tablet method, granules at the start and every 500 feet and 5-gram tablets at joints, fill slowly at 1 foot per second or less, hold 24 hours (48 hours if the water is colder than 41 F), about 25 mg/L; continuous feed, at least 25 mg/L going in and at least 10 mg/L left after 24 hours; slug method, at least 100 mg/L for at least 3 hours, start over if it drops below 50 mg/L. Flush the strong chlorine out within about a day, dechlorinate where the discharge rule requires it, then take bacteria samples. The water agency's spec sets the real numbers, and many are stricter.",S,U,'2015')
add('mfr-dipra-thrust-1','US','Thrust at bends and dead ends: example forces and soil bearing values (DIPRA guide)',
"Thrust restraint basics, summarized from the DIPRA Installation Guide for Ductile Iron Pipe (revised May 2015). Water pressure pushes on every bend, tee, reducer, valve and dead end. Example thrust at 100 psi: a 12 inch 90 degree bend pushes about 19,353 lb and a 12 inch dead end about 13,685 lb; thrust scales straight with pressure, so use test pressure, not working pressure. Example soil bearing values at 4 feet deep (Table 8): muck 0, sand about 4,000 lb per square foot, hard clay about 9,000. Common practice is block bearing area = thrust divided by the allowable soil bearing, poured against undisturbed soil. Blocks are concrete of at least 2,000 psi, cured at least five days before testing. Mechanical joints by themselves hold no thrust. Restrained joints (or wedge-action glands) are used instead of blocks where space is tight or the soil behind the block may be dug out later; the restrained length comes from the engineer or the agency table. Always build what the plans or the agency thrust block detail shows; these numbers are examples, not a design.",S,U,'2015','medium')

U='https://assets.adspipe.com/asset/a0e50e21-db64-418d-967d-7359e980d74e/TN-2-01-Minimum-Maximum-Cover-Heights-for-HDPE-per-AASHTO.pdf'
S='ADS (Advanced Drainage Systems) Technical Note TN 2.01, Minimum and Maximum Burial Depth for Corrugated HDPE Pipe (per AASHTO), rev. AB, copyright 2024 (our summary; read 2026-11-20)'
add('mfr-ads-cover-1','US','ADS N-12 corrugated HDPE pipe: minimum and maximum cover (TN 2.01)',
"Corrugated HDPE storm pipe cover, summarized from ADS Technical Note TN 2.01 (rev. AB, 2024) for N-12 pipe. Minimum cover under H-20, H-25 or HL-93 traffic: 1 foot over the crown for 4 to 48 inch pipe and 2 feet for 54 and 60 inch; flexible pavement thickness does not count toward it, and construction or paving equipment may need more temporary cover (see the ADS Drainage Handbook installation section). Maximum cover depends on the backfill around the pipe. Examples in feet for 12 / 24 / 36 / 48 / 60 inch: Class 1 compacted stone 35 / 28 / 26 / 25 / 25; Class 2 at 95 percent 24 / 20 / 18 / 17 / 17; Class 2 at 90 percent 17 / 13 / 12 / 11 / 11; Class 2 at 85 percent 8 / 7 / 7 / 7 / 6; Class 3 at 95 percent 17 / 14 / 13 / 12 / 12; Class 3 at 90 percent 11 / 10 / 9 / 7 / 7. The table assumes installation per ASTM D2321, no groundwater pressure and 120 pcf soil. These are general guidance, not a project design, and other makers' pipe has its own tables.",S,U,'2024')

U='https://concretepipe.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/07/installation_guide.pdf'
S='American Concrete Pipe Association, Concrete Pipe and Box Culvert Installation manual (our summary; read 2026-11-20). Standard Installations are the ones in ASTM C1479 and ASCE 15.'
add('mfr-acpa-install-1','US','Concrete pipe (RCP) Standard Installations Types 1 to 4: bedding thickness and compaction (ACPA, ASTM C1479)',
"Reinforced concrete pipe installation, summarized from the American Concrete Pipe Association installation manual. The four Standard Installations (Type 1 best, Type 4 least effort) are the same ones in ASTM C1479 and ASCE 15, and the plans say which type the pipe class was designed for. Bedding thickness under the pipe: outside diameter divided by 24, not less than 3 inches; on rock, outside diameter divided by 12, not less than 6 inches. Leave the middle bedding under the pipe loose so the pipe seats. Soil categories: I clean gravelly sand, II sandy silt, III silty clay. Haunch and outer bedding compaction (Standard Proctor): Type 1, 95 percent Category I; Type 2, 90 percent Category I or 95 percent Category II; Type 3, 85 percent Category I, 90 percent II or 95 percent III; Type 4, none needed except 85 percent if Category III soil is used. Lower side: Type 1, 90 percent I, 95 percent II or 100 percent III; Types 2 and 3, 85 percent I, 90 percent II or 95 percent III. Trench width guide: 1.25 times the pipe outside diameter plus 1 foot. Lay from the outlet end with bells upstream; clean and lubricate bell, spigot and gasket; bar and block works up to about 24 inch, larger pipe needs a puller. Put at least 3 feet of compacted backfill over pipe or box before equipment crosses unless it was designed for that load.",S,U,'undated','medium')
add('mfr-acpa-test-1','US','Concrete pipe acceptance tests: infiltration, exfiltration, air, joint and vacuum tests (ACPA)',
"Testing concrete sewer and storm pipe, summarized from the American Concrete Pipe Association installation manual. Infiltration test: only meaningful when groundwater is at least 2 feet over the crown; the manual's base allowance is 200 gallons per inch of diameter per mile per day at 6 feet of head, rising to 282 at 12 feet. Exfiltration test: base head 3 feet; single reaches can run over if the project average passes. Low-pressure air test follows ASTM C924 and is limited to pipe 24 inch and smaller; it finds construction defects but does not measure water leakage. Larger pipe is tested one joint at a time with air or water under ASTM C1103. Vacuum testing: ASTM C1214 for pipe 4 to 36 inch, ASTM C1244 for manholes. Visual inspection looks for debris, cracks, joints fully home and sealed, smooth inverts and properly set frames. The project spec sets the pass numbers; many agencies allow far less leakage than 200 gallons (the Ten States figure is 100).",S,U,'undated','medium')
